From Lagrange Multipliers to Energy Tokens: Privacy-Preserving and Verifiable Decentralized Energy Markets

Ouadi Maakoul

AI : chatGpt, Claude, Deepseek 

Abstract

The transition to distributed renewable energy systems demands market architectures that coordinate millions of prosumers while protecting their privacy, guaranteeing physical feasibility, and enabling trustless verification. This thesis constructs and proves the mathematical properties of JOUL, a decentralized energy market framework in which the clearing price emerges as a formal mathematical object—the Lagrange multiplier of a distributed optimal power flow—and is surfaced as a privacy-preserving, cryptographically verifiable token.

The contribution is primarily theoretical. We integrate three domains that have remained largely separate: distributed convex optimization, differential privacy, and zero-knowledge cryptography. We consider a radial low-voltage distribution network of heterogeneous prosumers, modelled by the LinDistFlow equations that capture active and reactive power, voltage magnitudes, and line losses. The market is cleared by a two-level gradient Alternating Direction Method of Multipliers (ADMM), where each node accesses only the gradient of its local strongly convex objective and perturbs it with Gaussian noise to satisfy f-differential privacy (f-DP).

We prove a privacy amplification by iteration theorem for the multi-node, heterogeneous ADMM setting: the output-level privacy guarantee strictly improves with the number of iterations. This yields the seemingly paradoxical result that the published token price becomes more private the longer the market is allowed to clear.

We then establish a privacy-optimality tradeoff theorem: the expected suboptimality of the final market clearing after T iterations is bounded above by an expression that depends on the f-DP budget, the network graph, and the Lipschitz stability constant of the dual variables with respect to load perturbations. We prove that this stability constant is exactly the global sensitivity of the clearing price to private data, unifying the privacy and stability arguments into a single object. A matching information-theoretic lower bound is conjectured and supported.

The token price—the Distribution Locational Marginal Price—is shown to be unique, Lipschitz-stable, and verifiable via a PLONK-based zero-knowledge Succinct Non-Interactive Argument of Knowledge (zk-SNARK) with universal setup. The proof system confirms that the token was computed from genuine local measurements and the correct ADMM update, without revealing any raw data.

No numerical experiments have been performed as part of this dissertation. Instead, we provide a comprehensive, reproducible Simulation Design and Projected Validation chapter. This blueprint specifies the test networks (including the IEEE 123-bus distribution feeder and a synthetic 2000-node network), the metrics, the software toolchain, and the expected qualitative and quantitative outcomes derived from the theoretical bounds. The design is intended to serve as an executable research programme for immediate post-doctoral work or for any group wishing to implement and benchmark the JOUL architecture.

